// TASK 4: Admin Panel Discovery
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Finding the Employees Only Entrance</h2>

    <p>Almost every application that has content management also has an administrative area for managing it. Because developers tend to reuse the same naming conventions, admin panels are one of the most predictable things to enumerate.</p>

    <p>An admin panel is not a vulnerability by itself. The real risk appears when that panel is discoverable and protected by a weak or default password, turning a login page into a direct path to full control of the site.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Platform or Pattern</th>
                <th>Common Admin Path</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>WordPress</td>
                <td>/wp-admin, /wp-login.php</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Joomla</td>
                <td>/administrator</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Generic custom application</td>
                <td>/admin, /manage, /backend</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Hosting or server panels</td>
                <td>/cpanel, /phpmyadmin</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> A Chain, Not a Single Step</h4>
        <p>An exposed admin panel paired with default or reused credentials is one of the fastest routes from enumeration to full compromise. Enumeration finds the door. Weak credentials leave it unlocked.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 5: Backup and Configuration File Exposure
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>The Spare Key Under the Doormat</h2>

    <p>Developers frequently leave backup copies of files on a live server, intending to remove them later. A file like config.php.bak looks harmless in a directory listing, but it often contains the exact same sensitive content as the live configuration file, just without any code execution protecting it.</p>

    <p>Configuration files are especially valuable to find because they typically store database hostnames, credentials, and API keys in plain text.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Filename or Extension</th>
                <th>Typical Content</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>.bak</td>
                <td>A backup copy of an original file, often unprotected.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>.zip, .tar.gz</td>
                <td>A compressed archive of the site or database.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>config.php.bak</td>
                <td>Leaked source code including credentials.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>.env</td>
                <td>Environment variables, often including secret keys.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>.git/</td>
                <td>An exposed version control folder revealing full source history.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Real World Pattern</h4>
        <p>A common scenario is a developer renaming config.php to config.php.old while testing changes, then forgetting to delete it. Anyone who requests that exact filename can read the database credentials inside.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

